@page "/media"

<CatHead Title="Media">
    An image whose size the app does not control, and a gallery of attachments. Long-form text
    from a source the app did not author is <a href="/prose">Prose</a>.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-image-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Photographs from Unsplash</span>
        Illustration only, attributed under each picture. They are the one thing this site loads
        from a remote host; <strong>the package still loads nothing remote</strong>.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.MediaFrame" Title="Media frame" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A bounded box for an image whose size you do not control. The <code>aspect-ratio</code> is
        what stops the page reflowing as images arrive — the box has its height before the first
        byte — and <code>width</code>/<code>height</code> on the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> is worth
        writing anyway, because it tells the browser the ratio of the file rather than of the frame.
        <code>loading="lazy"</code> on anything below the fold; a real <code>alt</code> always, and
        <code>&lt;figure class="media-figure"&gt;</code> when there is a caption.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.Fit" Title="Contain or cover" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The same photograph in the same square frame, and the whole decision in one pair. The
        default <strong>contains</strong>: the image is letterboxed and all of it is there, which is
        what a screenshot, a scanned document or a label needs, because cropping one removes the
        part somebody needed. <code>.media-frame--cover</code> <strong>fills and crops</strong>,
        which is what a thumbnail or a banner needs. Picking the wrong one is silent — the image
        still appears, just without the corner that mattered.
    </p>
</CatExample>




<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.Ratio" Title="Any ratio, one token" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Every frame that holds a picture at a fixed shape reads <code>--media-ratio</code>: the
        <code>.media-frame</code>, the <code>.skeleton-image</code> it replaces, a
        <a href="/form-choice"><code>.choice-tile-art</code></a>, a
        <a href="/files"><code>.file-tile-thumb</code></a> and a
        <a href="/video"><code>.media-player</code></a>'s video. Each is 16:9 unless it says otherwise
        &mdash; a file tile is square &mdash; so a skeleton swaps for its picture without a jump. Set
        the token on one frame or on a whole container: <code>4 / 3</code>, <code>3 / 2</code>,
        <code>9 / 16</code>. <code>--square</code> and <code>--wide</code> set it too.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.Transparency" Title="Seeing transparency" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.media-frame--transparency</code> paints a checkerboard behind the picture, so a
        transparent background can be told from a white or grey one &mdash; a logo, an icon, a
        cut-out, an upload being checked. It is behind the picture alone, so a letterbox stays plain.
        The squares are <code>--surface-soft</code> and
        <code>--border</code>, so they follow the theme; <code>--checker-size</code> is one square.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.TransparencyFrames" Title="Transparency in every frame" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Every frame that shows a picture takes the same modifier: <code>.file-tile-thumb--transparency</code>
        on a file tile, <code>.file-thumb--transparency</code> on an attachment waiting to upload, and
        <code>.choice-tile-art--transparency</code> on a choice between pictures &mdash; where telling a
        transparent logo from a boxed one is the whole decision. The lightbox's stage takes
        <code>.lightbox-stage--transparency</code>, below.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Media/LightboxTransparency.html" Title="Transparency in the lightbox">
    <p>
        The same modifier on the stage of a <code>.lightbox</code>, for a picture opened full size.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.Gallery" Title="A gallery of attachments" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.gallery</code> is a breakpoint-free grid of thumbnails. Each tile is a real
        <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> directly inside it — never a
        <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> with a click handler — and every frame is square whatever aspect
        modifier it carries, so a row of mixed attachments lines up. Which is the point of the mix
        here: two photographs somebody took at the dock and two files, in one grid, squared by the
        same rule.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.TightGallery" Title="Pictures sharing one frame" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.gallery--tight</code> packs pictures that belong to one thing &mdash; the photos in a
        chat message, the ones on a card &mdash; two across with a hairline between them. The frame
        around them has the corners, so the pictures have none. Three leave a slot; four fill it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.LinkPreview" Title="A link preview" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.link-preview</code> is a link unfurled: the picture, then the title, a line and the
        host in <code>.link-preview-body</code>. The app fetches the preview and decides whether to
        show one; the picture's <code>alt</code> is empty because the title already names the link.
        A <a href="/chat">chat message</a> carries the same card.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Media.Lightbox" Title="Looking at one large" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; open a picture.</strong> <code>.lightbox</code> is a
        <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> opened with <code>showModal()</code> &mdash;
        <a href="/modal"><code>ShowModalAsync</code></a> from C# &mdash; so the top layer, the
        focus trap, <kbd>Escape</kbd> and the inert page behind it are the platform's. The
        <code>.lightbox-stage</code> holds the picture as large as the viewport allows;
        <code>.lightbox-head</code> carries the <code>.lightbox-title</code>, the
        <code>.lightbox-meta</code> and the <code>.lightbox-actions</code> &mdash; download, open, close
        &mdash; and <code>.lightbox-prev</code> / <code>.lightbox-next</code> go to the neighbours,
        with the <code>.lightbox-caption</code> under it all. Which picture is in it is the app's
        state, and so are the arrow keys: one dialog whose stage the app re-renders, or one per
        attachment as here. A picture that opens one is a <code>.media-open</code> button.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>closedby="any"</code> closes it on a click on the stage beside the picture, as it
        closes any other dialog on a click on its backdrop &mdash; a lightbox fills the screen, so
        it has none. A press that starts or ends on the picture or a control closes nothing.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatSnippet Name="Media/LightboxDocument.html" Title="A document in it">
    <p>
        A PDF or a text file is a <code>.lightbox-frame</code> in the stage &mdash; an
        <code>&lt;object&gt;</code> with a download link as its fallback, or an
        <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> with a <code>title</code> &mdash; and the download link in
        the actions is the way to keep it. Name the frame the way a screen reader would want the
        tab it replaces named.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>
